<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New China</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a>; title changed c. 1949–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 没有共产党就没有新中国, Méiyǒu Gòngchǎndǎng jiù méiyǒu xīn Zhōngguó</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>&quot;, also called &quot;No Communist Party, No New China&quot;, is a song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>. It began as an answer to a book.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1943, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was fighting the Japanese invasion during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> published a book, China's Destiny, which stated that without the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> there would be no China. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> answered with an editorial in the Jiefang Daily on 25 August 1943 under the title Without the Communist Party there would be no China, which concluded that if China had no Communist Party there would be no new China.</p><p>The poet Cao Huoxing took up the theme in a song written that year at Xiayunling, at first titled Without the Communist Party, There Would be No China. In 1950, shortly after the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> changed the title by adding the word new.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song opens and closes with its title line, sung twice each time. Between them it lists what it presents as the party's services to the nation: that it toiled for the people and set its whole mind on saving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, pointed the people to the road of liberation and led the country towards the light. It credits the party with holding out through more than eight years of the War of Resistance, improving people's lives, building base areas behind enemy lines and practising a democracy that brought many benefits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song was included in the 1965 film The East is Red. A memorial hall dedicated to it, the No Communist Party, No New China Memorial Hall in Fangshan District, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, covering 6,000 square metres, opened to the public on 26 June 2006 and was designated a national patriotic education demonstration site in 2019. In June 2021 a musical road 587 metres long that plays the song was laid on China National Highway 108 near Xiayunling, where it was written.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New China” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New China</b>&quot;, also called &quot;No Communist Party, No New China&quot;, is a song of the Chinese Communist Party.</blockquote><a href="/songs/without-the-communist-party-there-would-be-no-new-china">Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New China</a></li></ul></section>
